I('witness_audit',[11,12], 'idea of the earlier run',44,[2,8,9],[], 'No authorial theorem: a comparison protocol and a theorem-by-theorem table.', 'Keep date of witness, independence of witness, and mathematical completion as separate tests.', 'An earlier-run research proposal, not evidence that all seven assertions have been matched.','agrees','The methodological proposal is retained and separated from verified publication identifications.')
I('rigidified',[13], 'unproved claim',46,[5,6,7],[loc(P,69,138)],quote(P,87),'For a sheaf of groups G with endomorphism f, f-rigidified G-torsors form a gerbe locally neutral under surjectivity of a Lang map; the automorphism sheaf of (G,f) is Eq(f,id).', 'Specify the site and local epimorphism. The noncommutative coordinate convention must be repaired separately.','agrees','The object, marked torsor and band occur on these pages; the notes do not themselves prove them.')
I('rigidified_algebra',[14], 'reading question',46,[6],[loc(P,110,133)],quote(P,122),'The displayed b^{-1}a=f(u)u^{-1} does not follow for a noncommutative group from ua=b f(u). The correct equation is b=u a f(u)^{-1}.', 'This run checks a literal algebraic error; it does not change the transcription.','agrees','The notes correctly isolate the invalid noncommutative simplification; an S3 certificate is supplied in the computation file.')
I('lang',[15], 'self-flagged step (a step he marks as unverified)',46,[7],[loc(P,140,171)],quote(P,153),'For a smooth group scheme G/S and an endomorphism f with zero differential, the Lang map is étale; with geometrically connected fibres it is fibrewise surjective.', 'Smooth means locally of finite presentation; the finite fixed-subgroup conclusion needs a separate finite-type assumption.','agrees','The author explicitly requests the lemma, and the marginal extension is more general than the finite-field special case.')
I('fixed_subgroup',[16], 'reading question',46,[7],[loc(P,153,170)],quote(P,155),'The fixed-point group of Frobenius is étale, and G/N maps as an open subscheme; finiteness is not justified for arbitrary non-quasi-compact G.', 'For a finite-type group over a finite field the fixed subgroup is finite étale. An infinite constant étale group disproves the unrestricted finiteness assertion.','agrees','The notes correctly retain the finiteness boundary rather than importing finite type invisibly.')
I('unit_frob',[17], 'unproved claim',46,[8,9],[loc(P,175,217)],quote(P,184),'Finite-rank locally free O_S-modules equipped with an isomorphism F_S^*E to E correspond to finite-rank F_p local systems for the étale topology.', 'The local-system equivalence uses GL_n and the connected smooth Lang theorem, not arbitrary disconnected G.','agrees','The corollary inherits connectedness from the preceding proposition; its vector-bundle specialization is explicit.')
I('module_finiteness',[18,19,20], 'idea of the earlier run',46,[9],[loc(P,214,217)],quote(P,214,216),'Does a quasi-coherent Frobenius-fixed module have to be locally free? Finite presentation suffices. Finite generation alone over a non-noetherian perfect ring does not.', 'Finite-presentation proof by Fitting ideals and the perfect-ring cyclic counterexample are earlier-run proposals independently rechecked here; no unrestricted infinite-rank theorem is on the page.','agrees','The author asks the question without a precise finiteness scope; the three notes concern distinct boundaries of the same question.')
I('relative_frob',[21,22], 'reading question',46,[11],[loc(P,234,276)],quote(P,244,247),'For a morphism locally of finite presentation in characteristic p, unramifiedness is characterized by a monomorphic relative Frobenius and étaleness by its being an isomorphism.', 'The diagonal ideal is sent to its Frobenius power I^[p], not generally its ordinary power I^p.','agrees','The theorem and its proof are present; the ideal-power notation remains an ink question, with a two-variable counterexample to the ordinary-power reading.')
I('projection_ver',[23], 'reading question',46,[13,22,27],[loc(P,332,337),loc(P,689,690),loc(P,737,741)],quote(P,332),'The projection X^(p/S) to X is called Ver in the early formularium; it is a base-change projection, distinct from the commutative-group Verschiebung later constructed.', 'Do not infer a group-law operation from the same name on scan 13.','agrees','The domains and codomains distinguish the two uses unambiguously.')
I('frob_functors',[24], 'unproved claim',46,[13],[loc(P,347,367)],quote(P,365,367),'Frobenius pullback preserves quasi-coherence, finite generation, finite presentation, flat modules, and tensor constructions, but is in general only right exact and need not commute with internal Hom.', 'Exactness over a noetherian ring is equivalent to regularity by Kunz; this is a later comparison, not a manuscript hypothesis.','agrees','The notes correctly distinguish general base-change properties from left exactness.')
I('linebundle_frob',[25], 'unproved claim',46,[14,15],[loc(P,395,448)],quote(P,437,441),'For a line bundle L in characteristic p there is a canonical F^*L≅L^⊗p; the relative Frobenius on projective space pulls O(1) back to O(p).', 'This is a transition-function calculation, distinct from the Picard p-torsion/differential comparison.','agrees','The formulas and projective interpretation are present on the cited pages.')
I('frob_iterates',[26], 'unproved claim',46,[16,17],[loc(P,473,512)],quote(P,487,491),'Twisting over a perfect field preserves the underlying scheme up to the base automorphism, and over F_(p^h) an h-fold twist returns the base structure.', 'An underlying-scheme isomorphism need not be k-linear; the following corollary is unfinished.','agrees','The note correctly rejects a stronger S-isomorphism reading and records the incomplete ending.')
I('cancelled_kernel',[27], 'withdrawn in the same text (he corrects or retracts it later, with both places)',46,[17,19],[loc(P,524,530),loc(P,570,580)],quote(P,528,530),'A preliminary proposition asserting a finite radicial surjective Frobenius kernel is cancelled; the later live statement identifies infinitesimal height conditions for affine groups.', 'Do not transfer all qualifications of the cancelled proposition to the later one.','agrees','The cancellation is explicitly marked, and the surviving replacement has a different scope.')
I('affine_homeomorphism',[28,29,30], 'reading question',46,[18],[loc(P,533,565)],quote(P,534,536),'A morphism of schemes which is a homeomorphism onto a closed subset is affine. The stronger manuscript wording is a homeomorphism, so the lemma applies without a finite-presentation assumption.', 'The first proof is cancelled; the replacement proof must be checked on its own. Hua was a previous run, not an independent source available here.','agrees','The wording is de préschémas; the deleted proof is not a withdrawal of the lemma. The live local-affine argument works.')
I('height_neighbourhood',[31,69], 'reading question',46,[19],[loc(P,567,581)],quote(P,578,580),'The kernel of the ν-th relative Frobenius of an affine group is cut out by the p^ν-th powers of the augmentation ideal; a corresponding pointed Frobenius neighbourhood makes sense without a group law.', 'Use I^[p^ν]; the ordinary infinitesimal neighbourhood I^(p^ν) differs in several variables.','agrees','The group statement and the explicit nongroup NB occur together; the notation must not erase their distinction.')
I('picp_units',[32], 'unproved claim',46,[20],[loc(P,584,602)],quote(P,584,588),'For proper reduced X over a perfect field of characteristic p, global units have p-th roots; the Kummer sequence therefore identifies Pic(X)[p] with H^1(X,O_X^*/O_X^{*p}).', 'Normality is needed in the following differential arguments, not for this global-unit step.','agrees','The earlier note separates the weaker reduced hypothesis correctly.')
I('picp_dlog',[33,36], 'self-flagged step (a step he marks as unverified)',46,[20,21],[loc(P,603,649)],quote(P,613),'The author asks whether logarithmic differentiation injects Pic(X)[p] into closed global one-forms and sketches an answer using Cartier-fixed forms.', 'For smooth proper X over a perfect field, Pic(X)[p] identifies with global closed one-forms fixed by Cartier. General normal X needs a careful choice of differentials.','agrees','The question on scan 20 is pursued on scan 21; the smooth refinement is separated from the unsupported normal-to-regular reduction.')
I('dlog_quotient',[34,35], 'reading question',46,[21],[loc(P,640,649)],quote(P,644,645),'The map K^*/O^* to Ω_K^1/Ω_O^1 is not injective because nonunit p-th powers have zero logarithmic derivative. The quotient must also divide by K^{*p}.', 'Even after this correction, replacing normal by regular is not proved by the page.','agrees','The example t^p in a discrete valuation ring checks the precise obstruction; the cancelled codimension-one text does not supply the missing argument.')
I('transpose_cartier',[37,38], 'self-flagged step (a step he marks as unverified)',46,[22],[loc(P,654,676)],quote(P,654,658),'The author seeks a differential-side transpose of the restricted p-operation D↦D^p. There is no naive O-linear transpose; the Cartier identity supplies a nonlinear correction.', 'The test D=∂/∂t and (tD)^p=tD disproves O-linearity. The word échouer/aboutir remains undecided.','agrees','The notes correctly keep the target operation and the polarity of the final word uncertain.')
I('height_one_duality',[39,40], 'unproved claim',46,[22],[loc(P,679,688)],quote(P,679,684),'The author proposes describing height-one group schemes by a cotangent module with operators dual to the Lie bracket and p-operation; commutativity should correspond to vanishing dual bracket.', 'The precise established equivalence requires finite locally free/flat finite-presentation hypotheses, not arbitrary quasi-coherent modules.','agrees','The note identifies the correct restricted-Lie direction while preserving the larger unproved scope of the text.')
I('verschiebung',[41,70], 'title, date or page count',46,[22,24,25,26,27],[loc(P,689,690),loc(C,2,77),loc(P,731,742)],quote(C,2,8),'The field-base Verschiebung construction occupies a complete four-page typescript at scans 24–27; it satisfies VF=p and exchanges with Frobenius under finite Cartier duality.', 'The title is now present in C46-01. The sender, recipient and date remain unidentified; a letter tone is not authorship evidence.','understates','The earlier notes saw only the final page 27. The attached Codex pages supply the opening title and algebraic construction; correct their former absence claim.',{41:'corrected',70:'corrected'})
I('oda',[42,43,44,45], 'idea of the earlier run',46,[20,21,22],[loc(P,583,691)],quote(P,689,690),'Oda compares the Dieudonné module of A[p] with H^1_dR(A) for an abelian variety over a perfect field; his proper-smooth Picard result uses a smaller Cartier-stable subspace. Rational p-torsion points alone do not recover the finite group scheme.', 'The Oda comparison and proposed ordinary/supersingular tests belong to the earlier run. The 1967 thesis/1969 publication dates do not date these manuscript sheets.','agrees','The earlier notes are mathematically relevant but do not establish a direct documentary dependency or a full Picard-group-scheme identification from Pic(X)[p].')
I('etale_frob',[46,47,48], 'unproved claim',46,[30,31,32,33],[loc(P,762,878)],quote(P,833,839),'Absolute Frobenius gives a canonical comparison on the small étale topos; with that comparison its action on cohomology is the identity, including the stated bounded-above derived formulation.', 'This is not a claim that geometric Frobenius over a finite field acts trivially. Even when F_Spec(F_p)=id the comparison on a nonconstant sheaf can be nontrivial.','agrees','The author constructs and characterizes a coefficient comparison; the notes correctly distinguish it from bare topological equivalence.')
I('topos_axioms',[49,50,51], 'self-flagged step (a step he marks as unverified)',46,[34,36],[loc(P,880,928)],quote(P,918),'For a fibred category with designated étale maps, a Cartesian relative-Frobenius condition implies equivalence of the associated topoi. The author asks about the converse and indicates a universal-base-change version.', 'The categories/topologies and representability hypotheses must be supplied before treating the converse as a theorem. The crossed-out topology-independence phrase is not retained.','agrees','The note correctly preserves the labels (a)=topos equivalence and (b)=Cartesian squares, and notices the cancellation.')
I('covers',[52,72], 'title, date or page count',46,[3,29,38,39],[loc(P,10,57),loc(P,752,760),loc(P,930,942)],quote(P,940,942),'The plan and cancelled title sheets record drafting topics, including a Steinberg heading; they do not assert a Steinberg theorem.', 'Do not infer dates, proofs, or a final chapter order from cancelled covers.','agrees','The source contains headings and cancellations only in these locations.')
I('bs_split',[53,54,55], 'unproved claim',46,[41],[loc(P,950,980)],quote(P,961,965),'An fppf form of projective space is smooth and proper with relatively ample anticanonical bundle; it is a projective bundle iff it has a line bundle of geometric degree one. A section implies this splitting.', 'Projective bundle means P(E), not globally trivial P^n_S; projectivity conventions over non-quasi-compact bases must be stated.','agrees','The statements and incidence argument occur in the text, with the splitting criterion proved or sketched.')
I('divisors_bs',[56], 'unproved claim',46,[41,57,58,59,60],[loc(P,968),loc(K1,153,219)],quote(P,968),'The degree-n relative effective-divisor functor on a Brauer–Severi scheme is itself a Brauer–Severi scheme arising from the symmetric-power projective representation.', 'The margin at scan 41 is completed in scans 58–60, newly available in the Codex continuation. Dual/convention issues are recorded separately.','understates','The earlier note correctly called scan 41 unproved, but the supplied continuation proves the functorial statement; update the dossier status.',{56:'corrected'})
I('bs_generic',[57], 'unproved claim',46,[43,57],[loc(P,1000,1015),loc(K1,157,169)],quote(P,1006),'A Brauer–Severi scheme which splits over the function field of an integral regular base splits as a projective bundle; the text also invokes strict local factoriality for an extension argument.', 'Use the étale cohomological Brauer injection under the precise locally noetherian regular/strictly locally factorial hypotheses of the cited result.','agrees','The notes properly flag the stronger factorial version for exact-hypothesis matching.')
I('bs_obstruction',[58,65], 'unpursued implication',46,[43,45,47],[loc(P,1003),loc(P,1072,1082),loc(P,1110,1132)],quote(P,1003),'A PGL torsor has an obstruction in H^2(G_m) to lifting to GL; the same cocycle is the obstruction to a universal Picard representative, up to the dual/convention sign.', 'Specify étale or fppf topology, distinguish Azumaya Brauer from all H^2(G_m), and check the sign through the incidence convention.','agrees','The obstruction is written on scan 43; the exact identification with the Picard obstruction is a prior-run proposal, not proved there.')
I('rational_linear',[59], 'reading question',46,[45],[loc(P,1060,1068)],quote(P,1063,1068),'Morphisms to projective space correspond to generating quotient line bundles; rational maps require a dense domain and an appropriate linear system, with base-locus data.', 'No unconditional correspondence with arbitrary rational linear systems is justified by the terse line.','agrees','The note identifies missing local and domain qualifications rather than treating the line as a complete theorem.')
I('systlin',[60], 'reading question',46,[45,47],[loc(P,1072,1076),loc(P,1109,1122)],quote(P,1074),'The map from systems of divisors to Picard classes is meaningful, but the phrase canonical section is not defined without specifying the empty system/convention.', 'Its functorial fibre is a projectivization of sections when cohomology/base-change conditions hold; that does not produce a canonical point.','agrees','The entry preserves the unresolved convention. An empty linear system is not silently made into a divisor.')
I('grassmannian',[61], 'self-flagged step (a step he marks as unverified)',46,[45],[loc(P,1074,1076)],quote(P,1074),'The author warns that descent data for generalized Brauer–Severi/Grassmannian functors may need an effectivity argument.', 'A projective homogeneous form descends under suitable fpqc projective descent hypotheses; this does not identify every vaguely specified relative linear-system functor.','agrees','The warning is present; its precise moduli object is only partly specified.')
I('picard_margin',[62], 'self-flagged step (a step he marks as unverified)',46,[45],[loc(P,1077,1082)],quote(P,1077,1082),'A poorly legible margin links projectivity, geometric reducedness, a Picard condition and noetherian/Henselian arguments.', 'No uniquely quantified theorem can be recovered without reading the scan.','agrees','The transcription itself has multiple uncertain words; this is a reading-dependent problem, not an established claim.')
I('linear_embedding',[63,64], 'reading question',46,[47],[loc(P,1119,1126)],quote(P,1122),'A monomorphism from a Brauer–Severi dual into a projective bundle is used in the splitting argument. Monomorphism alone does not imply a linear subspace; a degree-one pullback condition is essential.', 'The Veronese conic P^1→P^2 is the counterexample to the unqualified inference, not to the complete splitting theorem.','agrees','The inserted word is monomorphism in the supplied transcription. The note rightly rejects the older epimorphism reading and checks the extra linearity input.')
I('weakened_flatness',[66], 'self-flagged step (a step he marks as unverified)',46,[47,49],[loc(P,1124,1126),loc(K1,2,13)],quote(P,1124,1126),'The author asks whether universal O_S≅f_*O_X and finite presentation suffice in the Picard/linear-system discussion after dropping flatness.', 'Scan 49 gives a descent-type continuation; it does not by itself establish every representability/base-change assertion from scan 47 under the weaker hypotheses.','agrees','Retain the question and the newly attached continuation, while refusing to infer a general Picard representability theorem from one line-bundle argument.')
I('uniform',[67], 'reading question',46,[23],[loc(P,693,729)],quote(P,711),'The reused typescript states uniform-space axioms and an equivalent basis version; the composition condition must mean containment W∘W⊂V, not membership in the relation V.', 'Authorship and original context are not established by a reused page.','agrees','The mathematical type of both sides forces an alarm, while the transcription explicitly preserves the visible membership-shaped glyph.')
I('coxeter',[68], 'unproved claim',46,[42,44,46],[loc(P,982,998),loc(P,1030,1057),loc(P,1085,1107)],quote(P,1054),'The reused pages discuss Coxeter diagrams, conjugacy of Coxeter elements, and the elementary graph facts underlying the finite reflection-group argument.', 'Do not date or attribute the typescript from its reuse. The c44:7 parallel is a cross-folder lead to merge with its owner.','agrees','The entire three-sheet argument was read; the graph and permutation side claims are included separately in the claims map.')
I('pid_injective',[71], 'unproved claim',46,[48],[loc(P,1134,1152)],quote(P,1138,1142),'Over a principal ideal domain, divisibility of M is equivalent to exactness of Hom(-,M), hence injectivity.', 'The page gives a reference to a prior problem, not a proof; this run supplies a Baer-criterion proof.','agrees','The elementary reused-sheet claim is retained rather than ignored as off-topic.')
I('continuations',[73], 'other',46,[22,27,45,47,49,52,53,54,58,59,60,65,66,67,73,74,75,76,77,80,85,91,95],[loc(P,651,691),loc(P,1109,1132),loc(K1,2,305),loc(K2,2,495),loc(K3,179,564)], 'Earlier-run continuation leads, now compared with the supplied Codex transcriptions.', 'Several previously unavailable continuations are now present: the Verschiebung opening, the degree-n divisor construction, progroups, Brauer questions and cohomological calculations.', 'Completion is evaluated claim by claim; physical continuation does not mean every pending assertion is proved.','understates','Correct the former unavailable-sheet descriptions. New claims and doubts from the continuations are explicitly indexed in this review.',{73:'corrected'})
I('pagination',[74], 'title, date or page count',46,list(range(2,96)),[loc(P,2,1152),loc(C,2,126),loc(K1,2,305),loc(K2,2,495),loc(K3,2,564)],quote(P,2,8),'The Pro package and complementary Codex blocks cover scans 2–95 of cote 46. Lower-corner archive numerals equal scan number minus one; they are not authorial document totals.', 'Count scanned faces and logical documents separately, and do not assign an author or date to the reused typescripts.','agrees','All assigned file structures were read. The original entry describes its own Pro subset; this record adds the now-available complementary blocks.')
I('bibliography',[75], 'other',46,[],[], 'Bibliographical list of the earlier run, not an authorial manuscript passage.', 'Every reused bibliographical assertion requires an opened reference; old note labels do not transfer verification status.', 'Current verification is recorded independently in references.bib and the search log.','agrees','Retained as provenance; all cited results used in the present review are separately checked.')
I('method_audit',[76], 'other',46,[],[], 'Earlier-run audit of contextual sources and reading workflow.', 'The earlier run reports comparisons with Hua46.modern and RESULTS_STATE; these files are not among the present attachments.', 'Those comparisons remain prior-run reports, not independent checks in this run.','agrees','No visual verification is claimed: there are no scans, and the named prior intermediate files are absent.')
