I('haar',[], 'reading question',46,[50],[loc(K1,14,19)],quote(K1,16),'The reused p-adic sheet describes Q_p, Z_p and normalized Haar measure of p^n Z_p; a negative exponent in the coset decomposition reverses the subgroup inclusion.', 'The author of this reused typescript is unidentified.','agrees','Read the whole sheet; the sign and f/g slips are recorded as scan alarms.')
I('progroups',[], 'self-flagged step (a step he marks as unverified)',46,[51,52,53,54],[loc(K1,20,145)],quote(K1,69,70),'The manuscript compares proobjects in group schemes with group objects in pro-schemes, with an essentially-affine condition and strict epimorphic transition systems.', 'The exact categories C,C′ are not fully legible; separate the fully faithful construction from the doubtful equivalence κ.','agrees','Read the definitions, the lim-colim proof and the affine-descent continuation; do not promote the marked doubtful κ statement to a theorem.')
I('cartier_divisor',[], 'unproved claim',46,[61,62],[loc(K1,220,265)],quote(K1,224,229),'In a locally noetherian flat family, a closed subscheme flat over the base whose fibre is Cartier at a point is a relative effective Cartier divisor near that point, with compatible base change.', 'The local flatness and finite-presentation hypotheses are essential in the lifting argument.','agrees','Read and rechecked the Nakayama/Tor argument across both pages.')
I('topological_brauer',[], 'self-flagged step (a step he marks as unverified)',46,[63,64,65,66,67],[loc(K1,266,305),loc(K2,2,61)],quote(K2,28,33),'The author asks which torsion cohomology classes are realized by projective bundles or matrix-algebra bundles, at variable or fixed degree, and whether group multipliers admit finite-dimensional projective representations.', 'Separate topological, analytic and purely group-cohomological categories; fixed degree is stronger than arbitrary degree, and arbitrary infinite CW complexes differ from finite CW complexes.','agrees','All questions and the stabilizing relation were read. The literal common-stabilizer relation and the unrestricted projective-representation question have explicit counterexamples.')
I('weil_restriction',[], 'self-flagged step (a step he marks as unverified)',46,[68,69,70,71],[loc(K2,62,187)],quote(K2,84,91),'Restriction of scalars is compared with H^i, first for smooth groups and finite field extensions, then through purely inseparable norm groups and nonsmooth kernels.', 'Smoothness matters for the étale torsor comparison; the norm=pth-power formula also needs the extension to have degree p, not merely exponent one.','agrees','Read the smooth proof and the subsequent norm calculations, preserving the doubtful margins.')
I('h90',[], 'self-flagged step (a step he marks as unverified)',46,[73,74,75,76,77,81,82,83,84,85],[loc(K2,192,373),loc(K3,2,221)],quote(K2,197,202),'The author develops nonabelian Hilbert 90 for units of finite-dimensional semilinear algebras, cocycle twisting and a finite-field reduction to a Lang map.', 'The finite-field objection in the margin is kept as an authorial reservation; it is not a counterexample to the correctly stated Lang theorem.','agrees','The full averaging and twisting calculations are retained, including the noncommutative order errors and finite-dimensionality boundary.')
I('cyclic_cohomology',[], 'self-flagged step (a step he marks as unverified)',46,[78,79,80],[loc(K2,374,495)],quote(K2,441,446),'For a periodic cyclic action, nonabelian H^1 is expressed by finite cyclic norm equations and a filtered colimit; the author seeks injectivity and surjectivity criteria for a quotient sequence.', 'Transitions are indexed by divisibility. Twisted norm equations must be solved in the kernel with the actual norm value, not automatically 1.','agrees','The calculations are read in their stated order; the final quotient criterion remains incomplete.')
I('semilocal_h90',[], 'unproved claim',46,[85,86,87,88,89,90,91,92,93],[loc(K3,179,543)],quote(K3,194,200),'A semilocal generalization seeks detection of H^1(Γ,A*) by residue/inertia data and Sylow subgroups; a finite-module assumption is visibly cancelled and later proof steps still use finiteness.', 'The unrestricted statement is false. The invariant-residue surjectivity condition is a separate explicit question and also fails in general.','agrees','Every step through completion, twisting and nilpotent quotients was read; the exact loss of hypotheses is documented rather than silently restored.')
I('quotient_attempt',[], 'unproved claim',46,[94,95],[loc(K3,544,564)],quote(K3,555,561),'An attempted counterexample to quotient existence invokes a free locus of a projective linear action and infers affineness from a descended ample anticanonical bundle.', 'Taken as the natural PGL action on projective space, the free locus is empty; ampleness does not imply affineness. The intended subgroup or missing construction remains unidentified.','agrees','The claim cannot be used as a counterexample without recovering the uncertain group notation and replacing the invalid inference.')
I('group_components',[], 'self-flagged step (a step he marks as unverified)',53,[3,4,5,6,8,9,10,11,14,15],[loc(J1,8,195),loc(J1,242,273),loc(J2,2,119),loc(J2,275,330)],quote(J1,45,47),'The notebook studies neutral components, separation, openness, connectedness and irreducibility of group schemes over fields, including general groups beyond finite type.', 'Some general assertions are established later; several local remarks omit connectedness/reducedness or specialization/generization qualifications.','agrees','Read every live and cancelled argument, preserving the explicit correction on scan 10 and the questions on scans 6, 8 and 15.')
I('geometric_properties',[], 'reading question',53,[7,8,10,12,13,14],[loc(J1,196,273),loc(J2,27,69),loc(J2,120,298)],quote(J2,224),'The author lists field-extension criteria for geometric regularity, reducedness, irreducibility, connectedness and integrality, and tests them by products.', 'The logical equivalences need nonempty/connected test schemes and standing irreducibility assumptions that are missing in several literal lines.','agrees','The products, field characterizations and counterexample x²+y² were read in full; each missing qualifier is kept as a reading alarm, not normalized away.')
I('proetale',[], 'self-flagged step (a step he marks as unverified)',53,[6,10,11,15],[loc(J1,174,193),loc(J2,51,119),loc(J2,299,330)],quote(J2,308,310),'Finite étale and pro-étale schemes are related to continuous Galois actions; for quasi-compact X, the component space is represented by the maximal ind-étale algebra of global functions.', 'Profinite, locally profinite, discrete and arbitrary component spaces are different categories. The Galois continuity assertion for arbitrary X needs its stated topology.','agrees','The author flags continuity and extends the finite étale dictionary; the distinction between affine pro-étale and locally pro-étale is retained.')
I('cartier_dual',[], 'unproved claim',53,[16],[loc(J3,2,48)],quote(J3,28,35),'The notebook sets out characters and Cartier duality for diagonalizable and finite locally free commutative groups, with examples μ_n, Z/n and α_p.', 'A line concerning representability of Hom(G,G_m) is internally contradictory as transcribed; it cannot serve as an unrestricted theorem.','agrees','The finite-flat dual Hopf algebra and the examples are clear; the questionable general representability line is isolated.')
I('monoid_letter',[], 'withdrawn in the same text (he corrects or retracts it later, with both places)',53,[17,18,19,20,21,22,31],[loc(J3,50,267),loc(J5,76,113)],quote(J3,267),'A letter dated 15 June 1973 gives a two-point affine monoid with an absorbing idempotent, refuting the quoted assertion that every affine finite-type monoid embeds in GL_n; the reply rectifies it to M_n, and scan 31 proves the corrected form.', 'The signature remains uncertain in the transcription; do not identify the correspondent beyond its visible alternatives.','agrees','This is an actual within-text correction with a full counterexample, not merely a later external refinement.')
I('distributions',[], 'unproved claim',53,[22,23,26,27,28,32],[loc(J3,274,312),loc(J4,2,42),loc(J4,174,321),loc(J5,114,177)],quote(J3,274,277),'Dist(G) is used for the full functor of linear functionals on the coordinate algebra, with a boxtimes tensor operation and coalgebra/algebra structures; it is not restricted to distributions supported at the unit.', 'Infinite-dimensional duality needs the functorial or continuous-dual setting. Group-like elements also need counit 1, excluding zero.','agrees','Read the entire formalism; finite-projective duality is separated from unrestricted linear dual and product topology.')
I('coactions',[], 'unproved claim',53,[24,25,26,27,28,29,30,31],[loc(J4,43,321),loc(J5,2,113)],quote(J4,105,107),'Actions of affine monoids on the module functor correspond to counital coassociative coactions; submodules, quotients, stable hulls and finite-dimensional approximation are then studied.', 'Flatness gives exact tensoring, but arbitrary intersections may require additional local projectivity/Mittag-Leffler hypotheses; no such condition is silently inserted.','agrees','The coassociativity computation and every subsidiary submodule/quotient assertion were read, including the explicit question about uniqueness.')
I('vector_functors',[], 'unproved claim',53,[33,34],[loc(J5,178,300)],quote(J5,244,247),'V(M)=Hom(M,-) is represented by Spec Sym(M); W(M)=M⊗- is represented by an affine vector scheme exactly when M is finite projective, with duality and representable End/Aut in that case.', 'The proof of the necessity must distinguish arbitrary scheme representability from the affine representation it uses; the finite-projective conclusion is checked against the functor properties.','agrees','Read all product-limit and biduality calculations, without conflating the two functors.')
